          "source_text_sha256": "44cfd40fcb943713a84fdc5422ba42b8f87f59439cb9913087e502e0bd29dd9a",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "needs-review",
          "text": "Description: On May 5, 2014, at 6:40 PM, a witness and his friend observed a flickering white light crossing the sky from West to East in 20 seconds. Only one testimony was collected. The Calsky website indicates at 6:33 PM on that day the passage of the satellite \"Sinosat 2 Rocket\" (third stage that launched the Sinosat2 satellite) on an West to East trajectory consistent with the observation. A observer of the same object who posted his observation report on the web states \"The photometric period, by visual estimation, is between 4 and 4.5 seconds\" (see Sinosat doc) which is consistent with the flickering reported by the witness (4 seconds lit, then 2 seconds without light). The GEIPAN classifies this case as \"A\" for perfectly identified: observation of the satellite \"Sinosat 2 Rocket\". However, we note that the observation duration of 20 seconds is much shorter than the satellite's complete passage which lasted 6 minutes, but the witness only observed it on the uppermost part of its trajectory (above 75°).",
          "translation_text_sha256": "a0618c78260484ea0c78ae8e63acab62a01855d63ee365a92683e8362c2ebc79"
        }
      ],
